ready: 1
kind: module
id: retention
name: Retention
summary: >-
  Owns forgetting: strength and fading, dormancy, forgetting and recovery, protection, real
  deletion, expiry, and stack deletion.
notes: >-
  Expiry covers raw captures and events. Retention runs in the daemon's daily sweep and through
  owner and agent requests.
runs_in:
  - sys.daemon
  - sys.cli
  - sys.python
owns:
  - memory strength and status changes from fading
  - the forgetting lifecycle
  - purge and stack deletion
invariants:
  - A memory fades only when it goes unused; loading it is not use.
  - Protected memories and the identity core never fade.
  - A forgotten memory is recoverable until its recovery window ends, then it is purged.
  - After a purge or stack deletion returns, none of the deleted content remains in any Kernle file, and cloud deletion is queued.
  - An agent can purge only one unprotected memory it did not get from the owner; anything wider waits for the owner.
rationale: >-
  Forgetting is what keeps a long-lived memory useful: idle memories fade out of
  working memory so the ones that keep helping stay visible. Retention owns no
  tables because every row it changes belongs to another module; it changes
  them through those modules' entities under its own sequences, so there is one
  place that decides when something fades or is deleted. Real deletion is
  separate from forgetting because the owner needs both: a reversible way to
  drop something from view, and a way to make it gone.
